<Hub pillarId="scams-and-safety">
  <p>
    The expensive version of this topic does not look like a cartoon thief. It looks like a person who is kind for a while.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <a href="https://www.ic3.gov/AnnualReport/Reports/2025_IC3Report.pdf">FBI's 2025 Internet Crime Report</a> says cryptocurrency investment fraud was the highest source of reported losses that year, at $7.2 billion. A table in the same report lists 61,559 complaints and $7.228 billion for that category. Complaints that involved cryptocurrency at all totaled 181,565, with $11.366 billion in reported losses. Those are reports, not every victim.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href="https://www.investor.gov/additional-resources/spotlight/crypto-assets">Investor.gov</a> describes the relationship pattern: someone hides who they are, reaches out by text or online, earns trust, then steers the person into a fake investment. The <a href="https://www.cftc.gov/LearnAndProtect/AdvisoriesAndArticles/ATM.html">CFTC's 2026 payment advisory</a> adds the methods that often show up next: crypto ATMs, gift cards, QR codes, and a demand to keep the call secret.
  </p>
  <Figure
    src="/diagrams/scam-red-flags.svg"
    width={440}
    height={980}
    alt="Checklist of crypto scam red flags: a stranger directing the trade, pressure to decide quickly, a claim you cannot lose, a fee to withdraw, a paid recovery offer, crypto-only payment, a messaged link or QR code, and a request for remote access."
    caption="A one-screen pause list. The scam guide under this hub cites the FBI and CFTC pages each flag comes from."
  />
  <p>
    If money already moved, stop sending more. Report it at the <a href="https://complaint.ic3.gov/">FBI IC3 complaint site</a> and at <a href="https://reportfraud.ftc.gov/">ReportFraud.ftc.gov</a>. The full walk-through is the guide below.
  </p>
</Hub>
